**Project Structure (Senior-Friendly)**
- Unity folders:
  - `Assets/IdleMonsterTD.Core/` (interfaces, core services, SO base types)
  - `Assets/IdleMonsterTD.Gameplay/` (waves, state, enemy/monster components, factories)
  - `Assets/IdleMonsterTD.UI/` (HUD, panels, AFK)
  - `Assets/IdleMonsterTD.DI/` (`GameLifetimeScope`, installers)
  - `Assets/Data/Configs/` (`*ConfigSO`, variables, events)
- Namespaces:
  - `IdleMonsterTD.Core` (Services, Pooling, SO Base, Backend abstractions)
  - `IdleMonsterTD.Gameplay` (Waves, State, Enemy, Monster)
  - `IdleMonsterTD.UI` (HUD, Panels)
- Assembly definitions:
  - One asmdef per top-level folder (Core/Gameplay/UI/DI) to keep coupling clean, enable unit tests per assembly.

**Branch/PR Workflow**
- Branches:
  - Work from `dev`; one branch per issue (e.g., `feature/24-enemy-movement`).
- PRs:
  - Draft PRs into `dev`; include checklist:
    - Interfaces/impls present; DI registration done; SO assets or stubs; DevTestScene manual check; adherence to DI/no-singletons/no-Find*/no allocations in hot paths.
  - “Relates to #<issue>” (switch to “Closes #<issue>” once complete); 1 review required.
- CI:
  - Keep placeholders active; later replace with Unity build steps; add required status checks to `main`.

**Quality Rules to Enforce**
- DI everywhere; zero singletons; zero `FindObjectOfType`/`Resources.Load` in runtime.
- ScriptableObject-driven configs and runtime variables; GameEvents for decoupling.
- Pooling-first mindset; no `Destroy` in runtime loop; reset state on reuse.
- No allocations in `Update`/hot paths; avoid LINQ in tight loops.
- Backend through interfaces; never embed secrets; use `.env` and CI secrets.

